# Curriculum-new FarmShare profile.
EXPERIMENT_SLUG="curriculum-new"
# No DATASET_ID/DATASET_VERSION or staging config -- the corpus rebuild
# (plan section 4/5) materializes each metric's parent on scratch once,
# under CORPUS_ROOT (see common.sh), ahead of any training job.
TRAIN_GPUS="${TRAIN_GPUS:-4}"
TRAIN_CPUS="${TRAIN_CPUS:-16}"
TRAIN_MEM="${TRAIN_MEM:-128G}"
TRAIN_TIME="${TRAIN_TIME:-20:00:00}"

# RATIFIED campaign parameters. Pinned here because this file is the one
# place every submission reads, and both values must be identical across
# runs for the runs to be comparable.
#
# CONTROL_METRIC only selects which materialized parent `control` reads --
# control is a uniform shuffle and uses no ordering. It still matters: the
# three parents hold the same 5,857,887 documents but concatenate them in
# different orders, so chunk boundaries fall differently and a chunk in the
# mtld stream packs different documents than one in the flesch stream. All
# control seeds must therefore read the SAME parent, or the noise floor
# mixes two datasets. mtld is chosen because it is the only metric that is
# both length-independent (rho = +0.0052) and unvetoed, and because it makes
# control-vs-mtld comparisons exactly parent-matched. The cost is that
# control-vs-compression comparisons are not parent-matched; state that
# rather than spending three more control runs on it.
CONTROL_METRIC="${CONTROL_METRIC:-mtld}"

# Campaign seeds: Phase 0 control runs at 42, 69 and 12345; every
# single-seed arm at 42. Reusing 42 across arms is the point of finding 1g
# -- with the pacing tag stripped from the step seed, two arms at one seed
# share both their data stream and their initial weights, so arm-minus-
# control at a matched seed cancels the shared noise. Seed 0 is reserved for
# smokes (see submit_smoke.sh) and must not be used for a campaign run.
#
# Note 42 and 69 also appear in the DISCARDED August campaign. Those runs
# used a different corpus and different code and are not poolable with
# these; run_fingerprint.json and compare_run_identities.py both refuse the
# combination, so the coincidence is safe, but do not eyeball run names and
# assume a replicate.
